1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 { This is the Panov Botvinnik Attack, the Panov Botvinnik Attack is a
dangerous opennig,it has that queen's gambit taste for e4 and d4 players.As
you may have noticed white is voluntarily offering black a deal of initiative. White swaps of pawns and makes the e4 pawn an isolated pawn. But
white is looking forward for an attack against black. White would really want black to play the Horwitz against the PBA (Panov Botvinnik Attack) } 4... e6 { This is the Horwitz line against the PBA or improvised Queen's Gambit.
This blockades our LSB but at the same time we get the opportunity to turn
the position into a Queen's Gambit Decined } 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Nf3 Bb4 { [%anno "ShreksGonGiveItToYa", shreksgongiveittoya] Bb4 is also an alternative in which does come in handy if you want to prevent white from having a bit of a superior force in the center. Thus, this follows an improvised idea as the Nimzo-Indian Defence. Pin the knight and probably exchange it in the near future to loosen up white's central forces. } 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Bd2 Nc6 9. Bd3 O-O 10. O-O Be7 { [%anno "ShreksGonGiveItToYa", shreksgongiveittoya] There's really no reason for black to keep up with the pin since white has already castled. Black cannot exchange knight for knight because it will help white's isolated d pawn to be reconciled. The isolated pawn needs to be disconnected. Don't worry about losing a tempo. The bishop on d2 is also misplaced aswell. } 11. Re1 Nf6 { [%anno "ShreksGonGiveItToYa", shreksgongiveittoya] End of opening preparation, black can start to put pressure on the isolated pawn. Fianchetto the light square bishop on the queenside with b6 and Bb7. You can stack up the queen and rook at the d file aswell! } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd8d7,Bd8d4,Bc6d4,Bb7b6,Bc8b7,Bf8d8,Ba8d8] } *


