1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { This is the Advanced Variation of the Caro Kann. The Advanced Variation
of the Caro Kann is one of the most dangerous and challenging to play
against. First of all white gains spacious advantage. White also has enough initiative on the kingside to launch a devastating attack against black
just as can be seen on the French Defence. I recommend the principled and classical way to handle the Advanced Variation of the Caro Kann. Abbreviated as the ACK. Black gets to maximise his bishop on f5. Black gets a lot of counterplay on the queenside aswell. In the French Defence the LSB is
often a problem since it is a bad bishop. Black struggles to put out the LSB on the French Defence. Ive played the French Defence and I know the theory behind it. Sometimes in the French Defence black has to voluntarily double his b pawn just so he can get rid of the LSB in exchange for white's bishop or knight. Black do not sufer the same fat as the Advanced variation of the French Defence. } 3... Bf5 { Thus, this is the classical way to play this, the ECK will be met with
the principled and classical Bf5! In that case black solves all his problem
on the LSB. } (3... Qc7) 4. h4 { The Tal Variation Named after Mikhail Tal Himself, this move is usually
associated with the opening trap of the LSB. There are many combinations of
sacrifices in here so I recommend the move h5! } 4... h5 { h5 immediately occupies the g5 square, this restricts white from making
further progress on the kingside with the pawn storm. Plus the idea of the
move e6 to create weaknesses around the kingside would not work since black will just capture with the bishop! } 5. Bg5 Qb6 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 e6 { [%anno "ShreksGonGiveItToYa", shreksgongiveittoya] e6 solidifying black's pawn structure (and also transposes into a mainline)
Qxb2!? is quite risky and offers white feasible counter play against black queen side since the b file would have been opened for white's rook to take advantage of. } 8. Nd2 c5 9. c3 { [%anno "ShreksGonGiveItToYa", shreksgongiveittoya] Other options for white is the heavy theoretical c4! Which in this case isn't covered in this course because it would have been too long! However to make it up to you this is covered in my other Caro Kann course (Non-Interactive) So feel free to browse through the Advance Caro Kann in here! (To find the Tal Variation with c4!? Simply go to chapter 4 - Advance Variation - Time Stamp is with e4 c6 d4 d5 e5 Bf5 h4 h5 Bg5 Qb6 Bd3 Bxd3 Qxd3 e6 Nd2 c5 c4!?)
Now if you don't want to study this long theoretical variation and would prefer a less theoretical line but sound. Simply skip this chapter and move to Chapter 35 where instead of c5 Qa6! Is instead played. } 9... Nc6 10. Ne2 Be7 { [%anno "ShreksGonGiveItToYa", shreksgongiveittoya] Black should exchange white's light square bishop, of course white would decline! Well if thats the case then black should exchange bishops in the next move! } 11. Rb1 cxd4 12. cxd4 Bxg5 13. hxg5 { [%anno "ShreksGonGiveItToYa", shreksgongiveittoya] Simply exchange bishops, with no fear of a kingside attack! (Keep in mind black should not castle kingside since this would leave black's king vulnerable) } 13... Nge7 14. a3 g6 15. Nb3 a5 { [%anno "ShreksGonGiveItToYa", shreksgongiveittoya] End of opening preparation, black can start throwing a minority attack on the kingside. } *


