1. e4 c6 2. c4 { This is the Panov Attack The Panov Attack is an aggressive opening choice
against the Caro Kann. First of all white voluntarily creates an isolated
pawn which would be a clear target for black to take on. Second of all White is willing to create weaknesses in exchange for initiative and attack.
Even a pawn sacrifice would not matter if white has enough counterplay. Black's goal is to further fortify the position. Develop the pieces with ease and is looking forward to a great game. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 { Taking back with the queen is too slow since it would eventually be
kicked by the knight after Nc3. Instead play Nf6 to take back the d pawn! } 5. Nf3 Nxd5 6. Nc3 Nf6 { Instead of taking the knight which fixates white's pawn structure
(White's d pawn is isolated taking the pawn back fixes white's problem.) We
can play Nf6 an develop in ease. } 7. Bc4 Nc6 { The f pawn remains a target in here for white. Be careful of white's threats } { [%csl Rf7] } 8. d4 e6 { The move e6 needs to be played to prevent white from advancing his d pawn
forward despite of the fact that this does blockades our LSB. } 9. O-O Bd6 10. d5 exd5 11. Nxd5 Nxd5 12. Qxd5 O-O *


