1. e4 c6 2. d3 { d3 is a positional way of handling the Caro Kann, eventually white is not
looking for a quick or aggressive attack but rather would play a positional
and quiet game. This however may not be the strongest option for white but it is a quiet and positional response when it comes to defensive play. } 2... d5 3. Nf3 { There is a nice opening trap similar on the Scandinavian Defence.
Eventually the threat in here is after pawn takes pawn Ng5 and white has a
tactical sequence of move once the bishop is on d3. If black is not aware black would terribly lose a queen. Alternatively white can just swap
of queens after pawn takes pawn, position is frankly equal in there. } 3... dxe4 4. Ng5 { Another alternative whereas after pawn takes pawn Bishop takes pawn,
White has a tactical trap with Nxf7! Followed by Bf6+! Black's queen is
gone! } 4... exd3 5. Bxd3 { [%csl Rd8][%cal Rg5f7,Rd3g6,Rd1d8] } 5... Qc7 6. Nxh7 Nf6 7. Nxf6+ gxf6 8. f4 Na6 { Other Caro Kann Sidelines - Caro Kann, The Caro Kann Defence: Full
Repertoire: Chapter 8: Other Caro Kann Sidelines - Caro Kann,
https://lichess.org/study/O54iTuAF/upNBSrLO, } *


